# Overview
#
# Change narration describes the system as a change from an earlier
# version (`The parser now supports tables`, `Before this change, the
# walker visited aliases twice`). A reader of the code as it stands
# never saw the earlier version, so the comparison tells that reader
# nothing. The fix describes the system as it is and moves the change to
# the commit message. The rule sits at Recommended, because a tutorial
# narrates the progress of its reader with the same words.
#
# Expression
#
# Each branch matches only the change sense, because Go doc prose uses
# the same words for a sequence at run time far more often.
#
# The `now` branch matches `now` before a verb from a fixed list, such
# as `supports` or `returns`, and it needs a subject word before `now`.
# Harper's part-of-speech tagger marks each word with a tag for its word
# class. The branch takes a word with the tag `NOUN` (a noun), `PROPN`
# (a proper noun), `PRON` (a pronoun), `DET` (a determiner), `ADJ` (an
# adjective), or `NUM` (a number word, such as `three`). A proper noun
# is a name that takes a capital letter, such as `Kubernetes`. A
# determiner is a word that introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or
# `this`. `The parser now supports tables` therefore counts.
#
# `can now`, `does now`, and `now also` count anywhere (`the client can
# now retry`, `the parser now also reads tables`).
#
# `previously` counts only as `had previously`, as the sentence opener
# `Previously,`, or before a subject (`Previously the walker visited
# aliases twice`). The pattern takes a word with the tag `DET`, `PRON`,
# or `PROPN` as the start of a subject. `was previously` and `were
# previously` count only before a word from `named`. Each of those words
# names or ranks a feature, such as `named`, `hidden`, or `deprecated`
# (`the field was previously named port`).
#
# Code comments that narrate the edit that produced them belong in the
# commit message (`Before this change, the walker visited aliases
# twice`, `Updated to use the cached index`). The pattern matches `this
# change` after `before`, `after`, or `with`, and it matches `updated to
# use`, `changed to use`, and `refactored to`.
#
# `than before` compares the system with an earlier version of itself.
# `the old` and `the previous` count after `than` or `unlike` only
# before a word from `versions`, such as `release` or `implementation`
# (`unlike the previous release`). `the new` counts only before a noun
# from `surfaces`. Each of those nouns names a part of the interface
# that a change adds, such as `flag` or `endpoint`.
#
# False Positives
#
# `time.Now returns`, a sentence that opens with `Now returns`, and
# `deadline after now uses` pass, because no subject word comes before
# `now`. `is now` and `now has` stay out, because a test comment
# narrates runtime state with them (`the row is now consumed`, `the
# store now has two rows`).
#
# In `the previously cached value`, `previously` orders events at run
# time, so the phrase passes. Before a word outside `named`, `was
# previously` and `were previously` describe runtime state (`the
# connections that were previously connected`).
#
# `than previously` stays out, because a Go doc uses it for a value set
# earlier at run time (`larger than previously set with
# SetMaxReadFrameSize`). Go doc prose uses `the old` and `the previous`
# for runtime order (`unlike the previous call`, `greater than the old
# value`), so both pass before a word outside `versions`. `returns the
# new count` and `validate the new config` are ordinary runtime prose,
# so `the new` passes before a noun outside `surfaces`.
#
# `no longer`, `used to be`, and `has been updated` are left out,
# because docs use them for a state that changes in time (`is no longer
# valid after Close`, `records what the limit used to be`, `wait until
# the row has been updated`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# Passive voice makes the thing acted on the subject of the sentence
# (`the flag was hidden`). A passive form (`was refactored to`) also
# draws a finding from the Optional ProsePassive. `was updated to use`
# and `was changed to use` each draw a finding from that rule, and so
# does `was previously` before `hidden` or `renamed` (`the flag was
# previously hidden`). No guard separates the two rules. The change
# sense of `has been updated` is a passive, and the Optional
# ProsePassive already reports it.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 66 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 13 of 20 sampled
# hits narrated a change. Most misfires were the progress of a tutorial
# (`You can now conquer the world with Nix`) or a back-reference (`As
# described previously,`), which ProseBackReference also reports.
expr versions [version, versions, release, releases, behavior, behaviour, implementation, API, default, defaults, format]
expr surfaces [flag, option, field, argument, parameter, subcommand, endpoint, syntax, format, default, behavior, behaviour, setting, mode]
expr named [named, called, known, renamed, hidden, deprecated, experimental, required, optional]
expr main [([NOUN, PROPN, PRON, DET, ADJ, NUM] now [supports, accepts, returns, uses, handles, requires, defaults, includes]), ([can, does] now), (now also), ([was, were] previously @named), (had previously), (previously,), (previously [DET, PRON, PROPN]), ([before, after, with] this change), ([updated, changed] to use), (refactored to), (the new @surfaces), (than before), ([than, unlike] the [old, previous] @versions)]
let message "Change narration. Describe the system as it is, and move the change to the commit message."
let description "Flags phrases that describe the system as a change from an earlier version."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The flag defaults to five."
allows "The parser supports tables."
allows "Set returns the new count."
allows "The value must be greater than the old value."
allows "Unlike the previous call, Close returns an error."
allows "The entry is no longer valid after Close."
allows "The key used to sign tokens lives in the secrets store."
allows "Now returns the current local time."
allows "The retry uses the previously cached value."
allows "The row is now consumed."
allows "The deadline is now plus five seconds."
allows "The watcher fires only after the file has been updated on disk."
allows "The value previously stored under the key is returned."
allows "The call fails unless the entry was previously removed."
